{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.6402298d28","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) - 4$. What is the value of $g(-1)$?</p>","answer":["-9"],"explanation":"$g(-1) = f(2((-1)) + 1) - 4 = f(-1) - 4$. From the table, $f(-1) = -5$, so $g(-1) = -5 - 4 = -9$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.e68a170df0","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$7$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) + 5$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["3"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(2(1) + 1) + 5 = f(3) + 5$. From the table, $f(3) = -2$, so $g(1) = -2 + 5 = 3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.4bbe8317c7","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$9$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$1$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x - 1) + 4$. What is the value of $g(-3)$?</p>","answer":["11"],"explanation":"$g(-3) = f(-((-3)) - 1) + 4 = f(2) + 4$. From the table, $f(2) = 7$, so $g(-3) = 7 + 4 = 11$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.526adb99d8","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$5$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) + 4$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["6"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) - 1) + 4 = f(-1) + 4$. From the table, $f(-1) = 2$, so $g(0) = 2 + 4 = 6$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.20c63b797b","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$0$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 2) + 5$. What is the value of $g(-2)$?</p>","answer":["1"],"explanation":"$g(-2) = f(2((-2)) + 2) + 5 = f(-2) + 5$. From the table, $f(-2) = -4$, so $g(-2) = -4 + 5 = 1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.68f7413fbb","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) - 5$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["2"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) + 1) - 5 = f(1) - 5$. From the table, $f(1) = 7$, so $g(0) = 7 - 5 = 2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.635ee1a33d","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$11$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x - 1) + 3$. What is the value of $g(-3)$?</p>","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"$g(-3) = f(-((-3)) - 1) + 3 = f(2) + 3$. From the table, $f(2) = -5$, so $g(-3) = -5 + 3 = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.d198e72b92","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 2) - 4$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["2"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(-(0) + 2) - 4 = f(2) - 4$. From the table, $f(2) = 6$, so $g(0) = 6 - 4 = 2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.49938af735","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) + 5$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["15"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(2(1) - 1) + 5 = f(1) + 5$. From the table, $f(1) = 10$, so $g(1) = 10 + 5 = 15$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.f942c4bbcc","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$8$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 2) - 4$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["4"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) + 2) - 4 = f(2) - 4$. From the table, $f(2) = 8$, so $g(0) = 8 - 4 = 4$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.72b016c566","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$5$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) - 6$. What is the value of $g(2)$?</p>","answer":["-11"],"explanation":"$g(2) = f(2(2) - 1) - 6 = f(3) - 6$. From the table, $f(3) = -5$, so $g(2) = -5 - 6 = -11$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.fde2ce2b17","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$9$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$12$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) + 6$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["15"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) - 1) + 6 = f(-1) + 6$. From the table, $f(-1) = 9$, so $g(0) = 9 + 6 = 15$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.219d15bb1d","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$7$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x - 1) - 1$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["0"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(-(1) - 1) - 1 = f(-2) - 1$. From the table, $f(-2) = 1$, so $g(1) = 1 - 1 = 0$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.9ec9242778","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$12$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 2) + 1$. What is the value of $g(-1)$?</p>","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"$g(-1) = f(2((-1)) + 2) + 1 = f(0) + 1$. From the table, $f(0) = -3$, so $g(-1) = -3 + 1 = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.e2e618dd05","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(4, 2)$ and passes through the point $(1, 14)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["4/3"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 4$ and $q = 2$. Substitute the point: $14 = a(1 - (4))^2 + (2)$, so $12 = 9a$ and $a = \\frac{4}{3}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.307d978fee","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(2, -8)$ and passes through the point $(0, -9)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-1/4","-.25"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 2$ and $q = -8$. Substitute the point: $-9 = a(0 - (2))^2 + (-8)$, so $-1 = 4a$ and $a = -\\frac{1}{4}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.5288aa1948","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(5, -6)$ and passes through the point $(-1, -30)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2/3"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 5$ and $q = -6$. Substitute the point: $-30 = a(-1 - (5))^2 + (-6)$, so $-24 = 36a$ and $a = -\\frac{2}{3}$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.77c4395f70","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-3, -1)$ and passes through the point $(2, -76)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-3"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -3$ and $q = -1$. Substitute the point: $-76 = a(2 - (-3))^2 + (-1)$, so $-75 = 25a$ and $a = -3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.d355b46369","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(5, 2)$ and passes through the point $(3, -2)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 5$ and $q = 2$. Substitute the point: $-2 = a(3 - (5))^2 + (2)$, so $-4 = 4a$ and $a = -1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.302696b765","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-2, 8)$ and passes through the point $(5, -41)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -2$ and $q = 8$. Substitute the point: $-41 = a(5 - (-2))^2 + (8)$, so $-49 = 49a$ and $a = -1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.56547ddfe9","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(4, -1)$ and passes through the point $(1, 26)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["3"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 4$ and $q = -1$. Substitute the point: $26 = a(1 - (4))^2 + (-1)$, so $27 = 9a$ and $a = 3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.98d33d1317","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-4, 9)$ and passes through the point $(-1, -9)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -4$ and $q = 9$. Substitute the point: $-9 = a(-1 - (-4))^2 + (9)$, so $-18 = 9a$ and $a = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.2686d0518b","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-5, 3)$ and passes through the point $(5, 203)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -5$ and $q = 3$. Substitute the point: $203 = a(5 - (-5))^2 + (3)$, so $200 = 100a$ and $a = 2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.0cdb17f955","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(2, -1)$ and passes through the point $(0, -9)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 2$ and $q = -1$. Substitute the point: $-9 = a(0 - (2))^2 + (-1)$, so $-8 = 4a$ and $a = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.309a1c87a6","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-1, -8)$ and passes through the point $(1, -16)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -1$ and $q = -8$. Substitute the point: $-16 = a(1 - (-1))^2 + (-8)$, so $-8 = 4a$ and $a = -2$."}
